a live-action winx club that will  definitely include  bloom and sky alright netflix you've got  our attention  say no more bloom you can always count  on us the wings club is about  tough-as-nails female fairies who  develop  unbreakable bonds while finding love and  fighting monsters  but the live-action version is less  colorful and includes fewer fairies  so is fate the winx saga going to blow  our alfian socks off  or leave us at the mercy of the burned  ones  we all know tv has a thing for dark and  gritty remakes  but is it always a good thing when it  comes to the winx club  it looks like netflix is focusing on a  more dramatic and less eccentric tale  of a group of young fairies as they try  to develop their powers and reach their  potential  but we can't help but wonder does that  mean fate  the winx saga will have little to do  with the original show beyond concept  and will its characters captivate us  like they used to the winx club  came out in 2004 but the series  continued right up until 2019.  there are some definite things the new  series has in common with its  predecessor  the story will focus on bloom played by  abigail cohen  whose powers have just started to  manifest it'll take place in the other  world and alfia will remain the boarding  school where a group of powerful young  fairies can train to earn their wings  and the specialists will be in charge of  guarding the realm  from some new threat called the burned  ones in a press release  netflix described the series as being  about a group of fairies who must learn  to master their magical powers while  navigating love  rivalries and the monsters that threaten  their very existence  obviously we're excited for the love  stories since we're still obsessed with  the winx club epic romances between  fairies and specialists  need a hand girls  in particular we notice that prince skye  will be a part of the series and played  by danny griffin  we can also spot riven played by freddie  thorpe the series was created by brian  young  who previously worked on the vampire  diaries so can we expect a bunch of  romantic high-stakes drama set amongst a  group of young adults  most definitely but since the fairies  are the focus of the original series  you'd think you'd see all of them  included in the reboot right  well that's actually not the case and in  fact these fairies seem to be almost  entirely different from the originals  what's similar is that the girls still  have their powers  there's water fairy ayesha played by  precious mustafa  mind fairy musa played by elisha  applebaum  and light fairy stella played by hannah  van der west tyson  there's also a new earth fairy named  tara played by elliot salt  who seems to be replacing flora for some  reason and then there's one character  technology fairy techna  who's missing from the reboot completely  which is pretty disappointing  but even with familiar powers it's the  way they use them that's so different  their clothing hair styling and entire  vibe are obviously pretty different too  brian young did that on purpose though  because he wanted to give the series a  dose of reality  one major perk of a live-action reboot  is that they can cast real girls with  different shapes and proportions  very different from the original fairies  giant eyes skinny outfits and  impossible bodies but without the colors  and flamboyant magic  it can be hard to see where the wink  saga gets all its winks from  after all is it just a show about  fairies or is it a show about the winx  club  since it's not animated young also opted  to create the magical other world in  ireland  the irish landscape gives fate its  connection to ancient fairy magic  and kind of feels like hogwarts for  fairies especially with the darker tones  in royal gate  plus the historical and epic looking  alpha  modernizing the other world also meant  getting rid of some of the gender roles  that brian young wasn't so crazy about  in the original  he explained that in our show boys can  be fairies  girls can be specialists which we're  totally on board with  we can already see some of the diversity  on campus there also  won't be any mean girl drama amongst the  fairy friends in this one  as young wants to leave all the cliquey  stuff back in the real world  as for enemies if the first few seasons  of the winx club are anything to go by  we might see the tricks or even lord  darker  though it's hard to say this early how  much the show might deviate from the  originals villains  the burned ones are the only specific  monsters mentioned by netflix so far  and they're a brand new addition despite  changes though young seems to have kept  the entire purpose of the winx club in  mind when creating this reboot  he mentioned it's always the thing that  makes you feel different  and like an outcast that is your gift to  the world fate  is meant to be a story of inclusion and  finding your power  and isn't that exactly the type of  lesson the winx club taught us when we  were kids  never in my life have i've been so happy  to have a fairy friend  you mean a fairy with magic powers who  can pack a good punch if need be  a few networks but netflix in particular  have really been getting a kick out of  remaking successful classic shows into  darker edgier series  just look at riverdale or the chilling  adventures of sabrina  which adds some grit to animated or  childhood content  but does it really work in these kinds  of shows there's usually a huge focus on  finding the reality within fantasy  we often see regular people going to  regular high schools for example  or living within the real world but they  have something dramatically different  and otherworldly about them  even in riverdale there's a distinct  sense that this isn't the real world  it's way too crazy and with so many  strange and terrible things happening  it's a clear escape from reality it's a  nice idea to make it more realistic  but it seems like other than names and a  concept there's not much visually  connecting the wink saga  to the winx club whereas in something  like riverdale the characters are  obviously stylistically based on the  archie comics characters  while it's also nice to see realistic  bodies fate  doesn't seem to have as much diversity  as we would expect from the very diverse  group of fairies in the original  like why is there no latina flora why  isn't musa asian  and why is technica missing completely  it's clear to see that these are very  different girls joining forces  but it would be nice to see a bit more  color since cultural diversity was one  of the things the winx club did so well  another question we have for all these  remakes is why darkness  where's all the fun it seems like tv's  new obsession  is with being edgy and dangerous but  some shows are campy for a reason  and it might have been nice to see the  live-action winx characters look just as  eccentric as their animated counterparts  shows like this likely do these dark  remakes because they want fans who watch  something when they were younger to be  intrigued by a more grown-up version  for example the charmed remake was  touted as being for fans  but in reality it feels like a show for  young adults that most original fans of  charmed are  actually too old for now it might draw  some people in because of nostalgia but  most of its fans are a new audience that  might have only caught up with the  original charmed after watching the  reboot  but of course that doesn't mean there's  no potential in a completely remade  series  like charmed the winx saga could be the  next great show about women with  incredible magical powers  replacing the recently canceled sabrina  abigail cohen was actually in sabrina as  dorcus  so it's fitting that she could carry the  torch for the next great female-powered  fantasy show  for younger audiences who want to enjoy  a colorful anime version  well the animated one still exists this  remake is supposed to be for people who  want more and want the series to grow  with them  so will fate the winx saga connect with  the winx club audience  or will it draw an entirely new audience  and still be successful  we think it depends on just how deep  they dive into these characters  motivations  and whether or not the burned ones are  terrible yet interesting villains that  keep us invested in the story  the winx club is about friendship  finding your power and coming together  so if they can get this vibe right even  with a darker and grittier look then the  winx girls could find themselves  fighting live-action enemies for many  years to come  do you think fate the winx saga will be  a hit do you plan on watching  let us know all your thoughts in the  comment section down below don't forget  to hit that like button while you're  here and subscribe to our channel for  more  thanks for watching
 

